  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What are bevel gears?",
      "a": "Bevel gears are gears shaped like slices of a cone, with teeth on the sloping face. Two of them mesh with their shafts at an angle, most often 90 degrees.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are bevel gears cone shaped?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the small bevel gear spin faster?",
      "a": "It has fewer teeth than the big gear, so each turn of the big gear pushes it round several times. A 40-tooth gear driving a 10-tooth gear makes it spin four times faster.",
      "asks": [
        "Does a hand drill speed things up?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where are bevel gears used?",
      "a": "Bevel gears are used in hand drills, egg beaters, car differentials, angle grinders, some windmills and the drive shafts of some bicycles.",
      "asks": [
        "What uses bevel gears?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do I turn the handle round and round?",
      "a": "The handle is a crank: a lever on an axle. Turning it round turns the big bevel gear, and its distance from the axle gives your hand extra turning force.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the crank handle do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What turns the beaters?",
      "a": "The small bevel gear sits on the beater shaft, so when it spins, the shaft and the beaters spin with it.",
      "asks": [
        "How do the whisks spin?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Bevel gears usually connect shafts at 90 degrees, but they can be made for other angles.",
    "A car differential uses bevel gears to turn the drive shaft's motion through a right angle to the wheels."
  ],
